From 0dcbe032c81ba112374f854980004f16dce34368 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Enrico Bartky <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 11:14:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Measure item 4-4's premise, which re-specifies it before it
 is built

The item is an XL: inline small JavaScript callees at monomorphic sites. Its
premise is that a call costs ~250 ns, which item 2-6 measured and which holds.
What was never measured is the other half - how many of those calls inlining
could reach, and what fraction of the corpus's time that is.

Counting at the invocation rather than at an instrumented site, the seven Octane
suites make 6 194 758 invocations. Item 4-1's call figure is 4 239 232, and the
two are not the same population: 4-1 counts emitted call sites, this counts
invocations, and the difference is left named rather than asserted.

Building the counter is where the corrections came from, and its tests are why.
A call to a NATIVE builtin reaches the same entry as a call to a JavaScript
function and has an emitted call site, but has no body to inline - 2 292 138 of
the invocations, 37%, so a ceiling that counts them is inflated by more than a
third. And a builtin running a JavaScript callback does not use that entry at
all: JSFunction.InvokeCallback takes one `using` scope where the emitted-call
entry takes five and skips the executing-function and legacy-caller bookkeeping,
so merging the two prices a call at the average of paths that differ by most of
their cost. Callback invocations turn out to be zero on all seven suites, so an
earlier guess that they explained the gap to 4-1 was wrong. Between them those
two mistakes overstated inlining's surface by 72%.

Of the 3 902 620 calls with a JavaScript callee, 64.0% are made from a promoted
function - inlining's whole surface, since 4-3b established that a guard needs an
observation and only a tier-2 recompile has one.

Against a hand-inlined control in one run set, six rotated repetitions: a call
costs 142 ns before it carries any argument plus 17.1 ns each, and inlining saves
149 ns (method shape) to 170 ns (plain shape). So the ceiling is 2 496 760 x
149 ns = 372 ms of a 19 694 ms driver: 1.89%, before anything is lost to
non-monomorphic sites, callees too large to inline, the guard, or the generic
path that can never be dropped.

The same probe puts a marginal cached property read at 47.6 ns, so reads are
9.16% of execution time and the fixed call prologue 4.47% - the two paths phases
2 and 4 are built around are together under 14%. The arithmetic-only control loop
is 16.98 ns an iteration, which points at item 3-4 rather than at phase 4.

Nothing is landed for the item itself: the finding is that it should not be
started as written, and that a new item 4-5 - reduce the fixed prologue, which
reaches 2.5x the calls with no speculation, no guard and no fallback - beats its
ceiling at a fraction of the risk. InvokeCallback is a shipping proof that most
of that prologue is optional.

Adds CallPathDiagnostics (off by default), CallPathDiagnosticsTests (7 cases) and
--inlining-call-probe. Repository suite: 7 830 tests across 13 projects, 0
failures.
